    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A chiefdom joins several villages under a hereditary paramount chief who rules through kinship, ritual and tribute, without the officials, standing army or written law of a state.</li><li>Chiefdoms arose independently in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, and some, like Cahokia and the Hawaiian chiefdoms, raised great monuments and fed large followings.</li><li>Anthropologists named the type in the 1950s and still argue over it, while chiefs remain a recognised tier of local government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> to Samoa.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word cacique, which Spanish conquerors applied to native rulers throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, was borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, whose chiefdoms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> met in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in 1492.</p></aside>
    </section>

